{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's 'betting strategy' defense fails on Popper's own terms because it implicitly reintroduces induction through the assumption of stable risk profiles. To claim that a severely tested theory is a 'safer bet' than an untested one requires the inductive premise that the future performance of the theory will resemble its past performance (i.e., that passing tests yesterday makes failure less likely today). Popper explicitly rejected the idea that past success provides any rational basis for expecting future success. By defining rationality as 'minimizing risk' based on corroboration, the target assumes that corroboration has predictive value regarding error costs, which is precisely the inductive leap Popper argued was unjustifiable. The argument collapses if one accepts Popper's strict separation: if corroboration tells us nothing about probability or future reliability, it cannot logically inform a risk calculation intended to guide future action.","counterexample":["A theory that has survived 1,000 tests but is fundamentally flawed in a way not yet detected (e.g., Newtonian physics before relativity) may have a higher 'catastrophic error' cost in a new domain than a speculative, untested theory that happens to be closer to the truth. Without induction, there is no rational way to prefer the former as a 'safer bet'.","If the environment changes radically (a 'black swan' event), the 'risk profile' assumed by the betting strategy becomes invalid, demonstrating that the strategy relies on the inductive assumption of environmental stability which Popper's framework does not guarantee."],"premise":"Popper's rejection of induction entails that past corroboration provides zero logical support for predicting future success or failure rates.","successor_question":"If corroboration cannot justify a preference based on risk minimization without smuggling in induction, is there any non-pragmatic, non-inductive basis left for a Popperian to prefer one theory over another when acting?","target":"4e9a77ea1dd0ab9f54fee73d6aa1225b4bc83a26db3dab7f3cc1fddd0f85032e"}]}